# CVS08A — Explain v3 ("Learn" depth): BLS & ACLS I — arrest. The 2025 chain of survival, the BLS sequence, high-quality compressions and breaths, mechanical CPR, choking (adult and
# other groups), opioid overdose, the two limbs of the adult cardiac arrest algorithm, the CPR quality box, H’s and T’s, signs of ROSC, the arrest rhythms (VT, polymorphic VT/torsades,
# VF, AF/flutter), vascular access and termination of resuscitation.
# No mnemonics or hooks. Driven by qpack/CVS08A.md (31 question links; ACLS-16 polymorphic VT asked 4 times, ACLS-12, ACLS-14 and ACLS-20 three each). ACLS-01, ACLS-05 and ACLS-11
# have no linked question and get short explanations with [pattern] decode items. No Top-up cards for this deck. cvs_cardlayer.json lines and the ACLS-16 cross note were read and used.
# No `fix` entries exist for CVS08A in trace/test/data/qbank.json (checked 19 Nov 2026).
# Checked online on 19 Nov 2026 (AHA 2025 Guidelines for CPR and ECC, cpr.heart.org, Circulation Oct 2025):
#  - Part 7 Adult BLS: pulse check no more than 10 s (1, C-LD); rate 100–120/min (2a); depth at least 5 cm avoiding >6 cm (1); heel of one hand on the lower half of the sternum, other hand
#    on top; firm surface, supine; 30:2 before an advanced airway (2a); respiratory arrest with a pulse 1 breath every 6 s, 10/min (2a); tidal volume enough for visible chest rise, avoid hypo-
#    and hyperventilation; obesity same technique (1); routine mechanical CPR not recommended (3: No Benefit, B-R), may be considered where manual CPR is difficult or dangerous (2b);
#    compressor change about every 2 min; agonal gasps = arrest; C-A-B. Adult FBAO: 5 back blows alternating with 5 abdominal thrusts (1, B-NR); unresponsive → CPR starting with
#    compressions; remove a visible object, no blind finger sweeps (3: Harm); late pregnancy or cannot encircle → 5 back blows then 5 chest thrusts (1).
#  - Part 6 Paediatric BLS: depth at least one-third AP diameter, about 4 cm infants, 5 cm children; adult rules from puberty; rate 100–120; 30:2 one rescuer, 15:2 two; infant FBAO 5 back
#    blows alternating with 5 chest thrusts with the heel of one hand, abdominal thrusts not recommended (organ injury); two-finger compression technique eliminated.
#  - Part 9 Adult ALS: IV first (1, A), IO if IV fails or not feasible (2a, A); ILCOR review of 3 RCTs: no significant outcome difference, lower odds of sustained ROSC with IO (OR 0.89,
#    0.80–0.99); endotracheal drug administration REMOVED (low, unpredictable blood levels). Epinephrine 1 mg every 3–5 min; as soon as feasible in non-shockable (2a), after failed initial
#    defibrillation in shockable (2a). Amiodarone or lidocaine for shock-refractory VF/pVT (2b). Routine calcium, bicarbonate, magnesium not recommended. ETCO₂: sudden rise >10 mmHg may
#    indicate ROSC; CPR target at least 10, ideally ≥20 mmHg; intubated, <10 after 20 min ALS = one part of a multimodal TOR decision (2b); non-intubated, no ETCO₂ cut-off to stop (3: Harm).
#    No specific diastolic target. Sustained polymorphic VT: immediate unsynchronised shock at maximum energy (1, B-NR), ‘all forms of polymorphic VT are considered haemodynamically and
#    electrically unstable’; magnesium may be considered for recurrences with long QT (2b), not for normal-QT polymorphic VT (3); lidocaine/amiodarone/ischaemia treatment for normal-QT
#    recurrences; overdrive pacing or isoproterenol for pause-dependent torsades with expert input. TOR: BLS rule (1), ALS rule (2a), Universal rule in tiered BLS/ALS systems (2a); the
#    Universal rule uses the BLS criteria (not EMS-witnessed, no shock, no ROSC); none validated for trauma, overdose or IHCA. POCUS for reversible causes not well established (2b).
#  - 2025 adult cardiac arrest algorithm (cpr.heart.org PDFs 250527 and circular 250620): CPR quality box, 1 breath every 6 s with advanced airway, epinephrine 1 mg, amiodarone 300/150 mg,
#    lidocaine 1–1.5 then 0.5–0.75 mg/kg, biphasic 120–200 J or maximum, monophasic 360 J, 10 reversible causes. The 2025 algorithm carries NO ‘ROSC signs’ panel and no 40 mmHg figure
#    (that wording, ‘typically ≥40 mmHg’, was the 2020 algorithm’s). ‘ET’ in the algorithm now refers only to intubation.
#  - Part 10 Special circumstances: arrest from suspected hyperkalaemia — IV calcium, bicarbonate and insulin–glucose each 2b, ‘effectiveness not well established’; inhaled β₂-agonists 3: No
#    Benefit. Opioid respiratory arrest: give an opioid antagonist; opioid cardiac arrest: may be reasonable if high-quality CPR is not interrupted. Electrical injury: standard resuscitation (1).
#  - Chain of survival page (cpr.heart.org, updated 2025): six links — recognition and activation, CPR, defibrillation, advanced resuscitation, post-arrest care, recovery; AHA Top Things to
#    Know 2025: one universal chain for adults and children, IHCA and OHCA, and a separate neonatal chain of care.
#  - India: 108 ambulance, 112 unified emergency number (ERSS).
#  - ILCOR CoSTR ALS 2046 (costr.ilcor.org, IO vs IV, checked 19 Nov 2026): the three RCTs behind the 2025 review were PARAMEDIC-3 (UK, Couper 2024), IVIO (Denmark,
#    Vallentin 2024) and Ko et al. (Taiwan, BMJ 2024). The Ko trial was retracted by the BMJ on 18 Dec 2025 (randomisation errors; Retraction Watch 29 Dec 2025); ILCOR’s update pools
#    the two remaining trials: sustained ROSC OR 0.89 (0.80–0.99), no difference in 30-day survival, discharge survival or neurological outcome; IV first (weak), IO if IV fails within
#    two attempts (good practice). Part 9 also: central venous access 2b if IV and IO fail; AF cardioversion initial energy at least 200 J (2a), flutter 200 J (2b).
#  - AHA newsroom 2025 neonatal infographic: newborn chain of care has seven links. AHA/AAP 2025 summaries: infant FBAO chest thrusts with the heel of one hand; children now start
#    with back blows (previously abdominal thrusts alone). Reviewer check 19 Nov 2026.
# From standard references without a fresh check (confident unless said): Morrison 2006/2007 TOR rules (ALS rule adds ‘not witnessed by a bystander’ and ‘no bystander CPR’ to the three BLS
# criteria); LINC, PARAMEDIC and CIRC mechanical CPR trials; Brugada/Vereckei VT criteria; resuscitative thoracotomy windows
# (penetrating <15 min, blunt <10 min; EAST/WTA). Not verified: the ‘lungs’ in the CPR icon (ACLS-01), and whether AHA formally ‘retired’ the term
# ‘rescue breaths’ (ACLS-04; the 2025 text simply says ‘breaths’).

SPINE = [
  ("One chain: recognise and act", "Every arrest, adult or child, in or out of hospital, follows one six-link chain, and it starts with a 10-second check that sends an unresponsive, gasping, pulseless patient straight to compressions."),
  ("Compressions and breaths that work", "Blood flows only while the chest is being pushed 5–6 cm at 100–120/min with full recoil and few pauses, and breaths are judged by visible chest rise, not by a number."),
  ("Choking and opioids: before arrest", "Severe choking is back blows then thrusts until the patient collapses, when it becomes CPR; opioid poisoning is a breathing arrest that naloxone reverses, but never at the cost of CPR."),
  ("Name the rhythm, choose the limb", "VF and pulseless VT are shocked; asystole and PEA are not, and any organised rhythm without a pulse, AF included, is PEA."),
  ("Shocks, drugs, access, causes", "Shockable arrest gets shocks first and epinephrine only after they fail, non-shockable gets epinephrine at once, drugs go IV first, and the H’s and T’s are hunted throughout."),
  ("ROSC or stopping", "Return of circulation shows as a pulse, a pressure wave or an abrupt ETCO₂ rise; when none comes, validated termination rules, never ETCO₂ alone, decide when to stop."),
]

PRIMER = "Cardiac arrest is the loss of effective cardiac output; the brain loses consciousness within seconds and is injured within minutes, so every step is judged by how quickly it restores some flow. During CPR, coronary flow depends on the pressure difference between aorta and right atrium in the relaxation phase (coronary perfusion pressure), which builds over several compressions and collapses with every pause. Rhythms in arrest are sorted by one question, whether a shock can help (VF and pulseless VT) or not (asystole and PEA). The AHA 2025 guidelines, which this deck follows, kept the algorithm’s structure but changed choking, access and several BLS details."

