    ('c', "**Torsades de pointes: polymorphic VT in the setting of a long QT, with QRS complexes that twist around the baseline.** A prolonged QT reflects slow, uneven repolarisation; early afterdepolarisations then trigger beats, often after a pause (short–long–short sequence), especially with bradycardia and low potassium or magnesium. Drugs that block the potassium current (haloperidol, methadone, macrolides, sotalol, ondansetron) are the usual triggers. Magnesium 2 g IV suppresses the afterdepolarisations without shortening the QT and can be repeated; stop the offending drugs and correct potassium. If it keeps recurring despite magnesium, raising the heart rate by overdrive pacing or isoproterenol shortens the QT; amiodarone and sotalol lengthen the QT and are avoided."),
